# 3. Scale up

Website: www.foobar.com
The `www` record (A record) points to the virtual IP (VIP) shared by the two
load balancers.

## Infrastructure

- 1 cluster of 2 load balancers (HAProxy) on Server 1 and Server 2
- 1 server (Server 3) for the web server (Nginx)
- 1 server (Server 4) for the application server and the code base
- 1 server (Server 5) for the database (MySQL)

Every component (load balancer, web server, application server, database)
has its own server. The firewalls, the SSL certificate and the monitoring
clients of the previous design can still be used on these servers.

## What happens when a user visits www.foobar.com

1. The browser asks the DNS for the IP of www.foobar.com and gets the virtual
   IP of the load balancer cluster.
2. The request reaches the active HAProxy, which forwards it to the web
   server.
3. Nginx (Server 3) serves static files itself, or forwards the request to
   the application server.
4. The application server (Server 4) runs the code base and queries the
   database when it needs data.
5. The database server (Server 5) answers the queries.
6. The response goes back through the application server, the web server and
   the load balancer to the user.

## Why each additional element is added

- **Second load balancer (HAProxy cluster):** in the previous design, the
  load balancer was a single point of failure: if it failed, the whole
  website was unreachable. With a second HAProxy configured as a cluster with
  the first one, if one load balancer fails the other one takes over, so the
  entry point of the infrastructure is always available. It also lets us do
  maintenance on a load balancer without downtime.
- **Separate server for the web server (Nginx):** the web server receives
  all the requests, handles the connections and serves the static files. On
  its own server, it uses its own CPU, memory and network, and we can add more
  web servers behind the load balancers when there is more traffic.
- **Separate server for the application server and the code base:** running
  the code is the part that uses the most CPU and memory. On its own server it
  does not compete with the web server or the database, and it can be scaled
  (more application servers) and updated (new code deployed) without touching
  the other components.
- **Separate server for the database:** the database needs a lot of memory
  and fast disks, and it holds the most important asset: the data. On its own
  server it can be tuned, secured (only the application server can reach it),
  backed up and scaled independently (for example by adding a replica).

In short, splitting the components gives each one its own resources, allows
us to scale and tune each one independently, isolates the failures and the
security problems of a component from the others, and makes the
infrastructure easier to maintain.

## How the load balancer cluster works

The two HAProxy servers have the same configuration and use a cluster tool
(for example Keepalived or Heartbeat) that shares one virtual IP (VIP) between
them. The DNS record points to this VIP.

- In an **Active-Passive** setup (the one in the diagram), the active node
  owns the VIP and handles all the traffic. The passive node checks the active
  one all the time (heartbeat). If the active node stops answering, the
  passive node takes the VIP and starts handling the traffic automatically.
- In an **Active-Active** setup, both nodes handle traffic at the same time
  (for example with two VIPs or DNS with two IPs), and if one fails the other
  one takes all the traffic.

## Application server vs web server

- The **web server** (Nginx) handles the HTTP requests of the clients and
  serves static content (HTML, images, CSS, JavaScript). It forwards the
  dynamic requests to the application server.
- The **application server** runs the code of the website (the business
  logic), talks to the database and generates the dynamic content that the web
  server sends back to the user.

## Issues that remain

- The web server, the application server and the database are each on a
  single server, so each of them is still a single point of failure.
- There is only one database server, so there is no copy of the data if it
  fails (a Primary-Replica setup would solve this).
